Use **`WebApplicationFactory<T>`** from `Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing` to host your app in-process, then point an MCP client at it via the factory's `HttpClient`.

```csharp
// 1. NuGet packages needed:
//    Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing
//    ModelContextProtocol (the C# MCP SDK)

using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing;
using ModelContextProtocol.Client;

public class McpIntegrationTests : IClassFixture<WebApplicationFactory<Program>>
{
    private readonly WebApplicationFactory<Program> _factory;

    public McpIntegrationTests(WebApplicationFactory<Program> factory)
        => _factory = factory;

    [Fact]
    public async Task ListTools_ReturnsExpectedTools()
    {
        // The factory's CreateClient() talks to an in-memory server — no port needed
        var httpClient = _factory.CreateClient();

        // Connect an MCP client over Streamable HTTP
        await using var mcpClient = await McpClientFactory.CreateAsync(
            new SseClientTransport(new()
            {
                Endpoint = new Uri(httpClient.BaseAddress!, "/mcp"),
                HttpClient = httpClient
            }));

        var tools = await mcpClient.ListToolsAsync();

        Assert.NotEmpty(tools);
        Assert.Contains(tools, t => t.Name == "my-tool");
    }

    [Fact]
    public async Task CallTool_ReturnsCorrectResult()
    {
        var httpClient = _factory.CreateClient();

        await using var mcpClient = await McpClientFactory.CreateAsync(
            new SseClientTransport(new()
            {
                Endpoint = new Uri(httpClient.BaseAddress!, "/mcp"),
                HttpClient = httpClient
            }));

        var result = await mcpClient.CallToolAsync("my-tool",
            new() { ["input"] = "hello" });

        Assert.Equal("expected-output", result.Content[0].Text);
    }
}
```

**Key points:**

- **`WebApplicationFactory<Program>`** boots your real `Program` startup in-memory — no TCP port, no real HTTP listener.
- Pass the factory's `HttpClient` into `SseClientTransport` so all MCP traffic stays in-process.
- Adjust the `/mcp` endpoint path to match your `app.MapMcp()` route.
- You can override services (e.g., swap a database) using `factory.WithWebHostBuilder(b => b.ConfigureServices(...))`.
- Make sure `Program` is accessible (add `[assembly: InternalsVisibleTo("YourTestProject")]` or use a `public partial class Program { }` marker).
